1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,472
This is ECE 358, video 6, part b. We solve the DNS exam questions. Part a teaches the concepts.

2
00:00:09,922 --> 00:00:15,383
We solve the question bank, application layer, questions 26 to 48.

3
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:19,055
Then we solve problem 4 of problem set 2.

4
00:00:19,505 --> 00:00:24,497
The question bank has no official answers. The answers here come from the slides.

5
00:00:24,947 --> 00:00:43,229
Now the question bank. These are descriptive questions. The answers come from the slides. They are not official answers. Questions 26 to 28: what service does DNS give, what is the system, and why is it a hierarchy? Pause the video and try it.

6
00:00:43,679 --> 00:00:55,178
Question 26. DNS translates a hostname into an IP address. It also gives host aliasing, mail server aliasing and load distribution.

7
00:00:55,628 --> 00:01:04,012
Question 27. The hardware is the many name servers: root, TLD, authoritative and local servers.

8
00:01:04,462 --> 00:01:15,065
The software is the distributed database of resource records, and the DNS protocol. Hosts and servers use the protocol to resolve names.

9
00:01:15,515 --> 00:01:25,691
Question 28. Look at a dotted name, www.amazon.com. It has three parts. Read it from right to left.

10
00:01:26,141 --> 00:01:32,754
com is the top level domain. Its TLD server knows the amazon.com server.

11
00:01:33,204 --> 00:01:39,476
amazon is the organization. Its authoritative server knows the hosts of amazon.

12
00:01:39,926 --> 00:01:45,963
www is the host. The authoritative server gives its IP address.

13
00:01:46,413 --> 00:01:56,781
So each part is one level, and each level knows only the next level. No server keeps all names, and each organization controls its own records.

14
00:01:57,231 --> 00:02:04,719
Questions 29 to 32 ask what each level of the hierarchy does. Pause the video and try it.

15
00:02:05,169 --> 00:02:09,927
Question 29. ICANN manages the root DNS domain.

16
00:02:10,377 --> 00:02:18,910
There are 13 logical root servers, with about 1700 instances in about 170 countries.

17
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:32,651
Question 30. A root server is the contact of last resort. When it is queried, it normally replies with a referral to the right TLD server, for example the dot com server.

18
00:02:33,101 --> 00:02:43,021
Question 31. CIRA, in Ottawa, manages dot ca. Its physical servers are in undisclosed cities around the world.

19
00:02:43,471 --> 00:02:52,388
A dot ca TLD server replies with a referral to the authoritative server, for example the uwaterloo.ca server.

20
00:02:52,838 --> 00:03:08,049
Question 32. An authoritative server gives the official answer for the hosts of its organization. For a name such as www.uwaterloo.ca, it replies with the IP address.

21
00:03:08,499 --> 00:03:15,304
Questions 33 to 35 are about the local DNS server. Pause the video and try it.

22
00:03:15,754 --> 00:03:28,255
Question 33. The local DNS server first looks in its cache of recent name-to-address pairs. If it finds the name, it answers at once. This answer can be out of date.

23
00:03:28,705 --> 00:03:35,276
Else, it forwards the query into the DNS hierarchy and returns the answer to the host.

24
00:03:35,726 --> 00:03:44,921
Question 34. On Windows, open a command prompt and type ipconfig /all. Read the DNS server lines.

25
00:03:45,371 --> 00:03:57,851
Question 35. When the laptop starts, the OS runs DHCP. The DHCP reply gives the IP addresses of the local DNS servers.

26
00:03:58,301 --> 00:04:08,242
Question 36 asks for the main fields of a DNS message. Question 38 asks for the header length. Pause the video and try it.

27
00:04:08,692 --> 00:04:15,647
Question 36. The header starts with the identification. It matches a reply to its query.

28
00:04:16,097 --> 00:04:23,158
The flags say query or reply, recursion desired, recursion available, and authoritative reply.

29
00:04:23,608 --> 00:04:27,427
Four count fields give the number of entries in each section.

30
00:04:27,877 --> 00:04:35,685
After the header come the questions, with name and type, the answers, the authority records, and the additional information.

31
00:04:36,135 --> 00:04:41,063
Question 38. The header has 6 fields, and each field is 2 bytes.

32
00:04:41,513 --> 00:04:50,473
6 times 2 bytes is 12 bytes. With 8 bits per byte, that is 96 bits. It is the same for a query and a reply.

33
00:04:50,923 --> 00:04:58,368
Questions 37, 39, 40 and 41 are about the query types. Pause the video and try it.

34
00:04:58,818 --> 00:05:04,301
Question 37. The two types are recursive queries and iterative queries.

35
00:05:04,751 --> 00:05:11,300
Question 39. Name resolution is the translation of a name into its IP address.

36
00:05:11,750 --> 00:05:23,825
Question 40. The syntactic difference is the RD flag in the header. A recursive query has RD equal to 1. An iterative query has RD equal to 0.

37
00:05:24,275 --> 00:05:31,336
The semantic difference is the reply. For a recursive query, the resolver returns the final answer.

38
00:05:31,786 --> 00:05:41,663
For an iterative query, the server returns a referral, or the IP address if it knows it. The client then asks the next server itself.

39
00:05:42,113 --> 00:05:51,905
Question 41. With a recursive query, the local server does all the work. The host sends one query and gets one final answer.

40
00:05:52,355 --> 00:05:58,073
This is simple for the host, and fast, because the local server and its cache are close.

41
00:05:58,523 --> 00:06:02,640
Questions 42 and 43. Pause the video and try it.

42
00:06:03,090 --> 00:06:11,410
Question 42. Yes. Server B can decline the recursion. It sets RA to 0 and returns a referral.

43
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:24,297
For example, A is the local server dns.uwaterloo.ca, and B is a root server. B replies with a referral to the dot edu TLD server.

44
00:06:24,747 --> 00:06:35,094
B does this because recursion puts the burden of resolution on B. A root server gets very many queries, so it must keep its work per query small.

45
00:06:35,544 --> 00:06:44,717
Question 43. The additional information section carries helpful records. Take an MX query for uwaterloo.ca.

46
00:06:45,167 --> 00:06:48,538
The answer section gives the name of the mail server.

47
00:06:48,988 --> 00:06:54,748
The additional section gives the type A record of the mail server, with its IP address.

48
00:06:55,198 --> 00:06:59,315
So the client does not need a second query for the IP address.

49
00:06:59,765 --> 00:07:05,205
Questions 44 and 45 are about the records. Pause the video and try it.

50
00:07:05,655 --> 00:07:10,413
Question 44. Type A maps a hostname to an IP address.

51
00:07:10,863 --> 00:07:14,724
Type NS maps a domain to its authoritative name server.

52
00:07:15,174 --> 00:07:18,694
Type CNAME maps an alias to the canonical name.

53
00:07:19,144 --> 00:07:22,259
Type MX gives the mail server for a name.

54
00:07:22,709 --> 00:07:36,469
Question 45. Records become stale because of caching. A server caches a record with a TTL, for example 3600 seconds. These numbers are an added example.

55
00:07:36,919 --> 00:07:45,580
Then the host changes its IP address, 1200 seconds after caching. The cached copy does not change.

56
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:55,417
So the record is stale for 3600 minus 1200 seconds. That is 2400 seconds.

57
00:07:55,867 --> 00:08:05,488
A host that gets this record receives the old IP address, and cannot reach the named host. The slides call DNS best-effort.

58
00:08:05,938 --> 00:08:19,293
Stale records are removed by the TTL. At 3600 seconds, the entry times out and the server deletes it. The next query then gets the new mapping from the hierarchy.

59
00:08:19,743 --> 00:08:38,708
Question 46. Assume that the local DNS server is faulty. It treats a received recursive query as an iterative query. Redraw the two workflows of slides 40 and 41. Pause the video and try it. This is our own answer, not an official one.

60
00:08:39,158 --> 00:08:45,558
We assume that the faulty server replies with its best information: a referral to a root server.

61
00:08:46,008 --> 00:08:53,752
First, the workflow of slide 40. Message 1. The host sends its recursive query to the local server.

62
00:08:54,202 --> 00:09:00,325
Message 2. The local server treats it as iterative, and replies: ask a root server.

63
00:09:00,775 --> 00:09:04,935
Message 3. The host now queries the root server itself.

64
00:09:05,385 --> 00:09:11,166
Message 4. The root replies with a referral to the dot edu TLD server.

65
00:09:11,616 --> 00:09:15,307
Message 5. The host queries the TLD server.

66
00:09:15,757 --> 00:09:23,757
Message 6. The TLD server refers the host to dns.cs.umass.edu.

67
00:09:24,207 --> 00:09:28,025
Message 7. The host queries the authoritative server.

68
00:09:28,475 --> 00:09:33,681
Message 8. The authoritative server returns the IP address to the host.

69
00:09:34,131 --> 00:09:43,688
There are still 8 messages. But now the host, not the local server, does the iteration. And the local server caches nothing for the next query.

70
00:09:44,138 --> 00:09:51,541
Now the workflow of slide 41, the full recursion. The faulty local server is the only change.

71
00:09:51,991 --> 00:09:56,812
Message 1. The host sends the recursive query to the local server.

72
00:09:57,262 --> 00:10:02,083
Message 2. The local server replies with a referral to a root server.

73
00:10:02,533 --> 00:10:07,184
Message 3. The host sends the recursive query to the root server.

74
00:10:07,634 --> 00:10:12,029
Message 4. The root server sends it on to the TLD server.

75
00:10:12,479 --> 00:10:17,492
Message 5. The TLD server sends it to the authoritative server.

76
00:10:17,942 --> 00:10:24,342
Message 6. The authoritative server returns the IP address to the TLD server.

77
00:10:24,792 --> 00:10:29,336
Message 7. The TLD server returns it to the root server.

78
00:10:29,786 --> 00:10:34,202
Message 8. The root server gives the IP address to the host.

79
00:10:34,652 --> 00:10:43,655
Again there are 8 messages. The root server now answers the host directly, and the local server is not used after message 2.

80
00:10:44,105 --> 00:11:04,947
Question 47. You start a company, Arctic Climate Consulting. You want a web page at www.acc.ca, and email addresses at acc.ca. List the DNS steps. Pause the video and try it. We use the method of slide 46.

81
00:11:05,397 --> 00:11:10,688
Step a. Register the name acc.ca at a DNS registrar.

82
00:11:11,138 --> 00:11:28,717
Give the names and IP addresses of your authoritative servers. We assume the primary server dns1.acc.ca, at 192.0.2.1. These names and addresses are our own example.

83
00:11:29,167 --> 00:11:40,495
The registrar inserts an NS record into the dot ca TLD server: acc.ca is served by dns1.acc.ca.

84
00:11:40,945 --> 00:11:51,782
It also inserts an A record with the address of dns1.acc.ca, 192.0.2.1.

85
00:11:52,232 --> 00:12:08,680
Step b. Set up the authoritative server at 192.0.2.1. Add an A record for the web server, web1.acc.ca, at 192.0.2.10.

86
00:12:09,130 --> 00:12:16,981
For email, add an MX record: the mail server of acc.ca is mail.acc.ca.

87
00:12:17,431 --> 00:12:26,604
And add an A record with the address of mail.acc.ca, 192.0.2.20.

88
00:12:27,054 --> 00:12:38,019
Now add the CNAME record. www.acc.ca is an alias, and web1.acc.ca is the canonical name.

89
00:12:38,469 --> 00:12:53,296
Check that the CNAME record is used. A browser resolves www.acc.ca. The dot ca TLD server refers it to dns1.acc.ca.

90
00:12:53,746 --> 00:13:07,079
dns1 returns the CNAME record and the A record of web1.acc.ca. The browser then connects to 192.0.2.10.

91
00:13:07,529 --> 00:13:20,863
Question 48. DNS is an application-layer protocol, but it handles IP addresses, a network-layer concept. What is the main advantage? Pause the video and try it.

92
00:13:21,313 --> 00:13:28,225
Look at where DNS runs. Only the hosts and the DNS servers at the edge run DNS.

93
00:13:28,675 --> 00:13:34,499
The routers in the core only forward IP datagrams. They do not know about names.

94
00:13:34,949 --> 00:13:41,839
So the advantage is complexity at the network's edge, as slide 30 says. The core stays simple.

95
00:13:42,289 --> 00:13:48,007
DNS can change, grow and add new records without any change to the routers.

96
00:13:48,457 --> 00:14:16,830
Problem set 2, problem 4. You click on a link. The IP address is not cached, so a DNS lookup is needed. n DNS servers are visited, with round trip times RTT1 to RTTn. The page is one small HTML object. RTT0 is the RTT to the Web server, and the transmission time is zero. How long until the object arrives? Pause the video and try it.

97
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:30,528
First, DNS. Each visit is one query and one reply, so it costs one RTT. The DNS time is RTT1 plus RTT2, up to RTTn.

98
00:14:30,978 --> 00:14:39,831
Then HTTP, with the response time of slide 22. One RTT0 opens the TCP connection.

99
00:14:40,281 --> 00:14:51,631
A second RTT0 carries the request and returns the object. The transmission time is zero. So the HTTP time is 2 RTT0.

100
00:14:52,081 --> 00:15:00,614
The total is 2 RTT0 plus RTT1, up to RTTn. This matches the official solution.

101
00:15:01,064 --> 00:15:12,221
Now an example with added values. n is 3, with RTTs of 10, 30 and 20 milliseconds. RTT0 is 50 milliseconds.

102
00:15:12,671 --> 00:15:18,090
The DNS part is 10 plus 30 plus 20. That is 60 milliseconds.

103
00:15:18,540 --> 00:15:23,916
The HTTP part is 2 times 50. That is 100 milliseconds.

104
00:15:24,366 --> 00:15:29,571
The total is 60 plus 100. That is 160 milliseconds.

105
00:15:30,021 --> 00:15:37,851
DNS comes first, because the browser needs the IP address before it can open the TCP connection.

106
00:15:38,301 --> 00:15:53,789
Here is the recap of part b. One. For descriptive questions, answer with the terms of the slides: root, TLD, authoritative and local servers, the RD and RA flags, the record types, and the TTL.

107
00:15:54,239 --> 00:15:59,380
Two. The DNS header has 6 fields of 2 bytes. That is 12 bytes.

108
00:15:59,830 --> 00:16:06,379
Three. To redraw a workflow, number every message, and check that each query has its reply.

109
00:16:06,829 --> 00:16:18,627
Four. For a new domain, the registrar adds NS and A records to the TLD server, and you add A, MX and CNAME records to your authoritative server.

110
00:16:19,077 --> 00:16:32,879
Five. With a DNS lookup, the time to get a page is the sum of the DNS RTTs plus 2 RTT0, plus the file transmission time. That is the end of video 6.
